A single hotel's FF&E order might touch factories in Dongguan, showrooms in Dubai, and a
site team in Lagos — all before a single item reaches the room it was designed for. At that
scale, the hard problems are rarely about any one item.

## Currency and duty are part of the design conversation

A finish that is cost-effective when specified can become the most expensive line in the
budget by the time duty, freight and currency movement are added — which means procurement
has to be in the room during design, not just after it.

## Consolidation is a discipline, not an afterthought

Shipping items separately as they finish production feels faster but rarely is — it
multiplies customs entries, freight costs and the chance that one delayed container holds up
an entire floor. Planning consolidation points from the start keeps a five-country order
moving as one shipment, not twenty.

## Local knowledge is not optional

Import regulations, labour availability and site access vary enormously by market. Having a
team on the ground in the destination country — not just at the origin factory — is what
turns "we shipped it" into "it arrived, cleared, and got installed."

The properties that open on schedule are rarely the ones with the simplest sourcing. They
are the ones where the complexity was planned for from the first drawing.
